NEXT: BEST GUESS -- and it is a candidate, explicitly NOT a recommendation.

CANDIDATE: the test is bare, and a bare field name in this codebase resolves
against the most-recently-mentioned field rather than against the action's
argument. generateParse mentions several fields before it reaches the
connective choice, so `hasAttributeS` may be answering about the wrong node
entirely -- which would explain a discriminant that never once selects OR.

WHAT WOULD PROMOTE IT: print the same bare test beside an explicit
`argument.hasAttributeS` in the same position inside generateParse, on a rule
known to carry zero attributes. Two different answers confirm it. ROW 2 of this
file runs the bare test from a DIFFERENT action, so it is suggestive and is not
that measurement -- the resolution context is what is in question, so the test
has to be read where the defect lives.

WHAT WOULD KILL IT: the two spellings agreeing. Then the discriminant is being
answered correctly and the defect is in what it is being ASKED -- attributes
may simply be the wrong question for sequence-versus-alternation, and the
charter becomes which property actually separates the two.

GRADE: BEST GUESS. No ruling has been issued on the fix shape and no build has
landed, so this is neither RULED nor REMEDY.
